|segment[ver-demonstrated-is-in-the-action-space] :type mechanism :status discussion-grade :stage draft
  :max-attainable empirical
  :tags [virtues action-space transmission]
  :from .to-integrate/successor-session-quotes.md
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-2.md§V

  |title Knowing a thing has been done by a peer changes the action space, which an instruction cannot

  |summary A demonstrated success by someone at one's own capability level makes
    a move available that was not available before — a different effect from
    being told the move is correct, and often the load-bearing one.

  |formal-expression
    An instruction asserts that a move is *right*. A demonstration establishes
    that the move is *reachable*, by an entity of roughly this capability, under
    roughly these conditions. Those are different facts and they enter at
    different places: the first is a claim to be evaluated, the second is a
    change in what the option set contains.

    The consequence is asymmetric with the failure case. A record of failures
    supports *avoidance* — it says which moves cost something. It cannot say
    which move to make, because a failure does not exhibit its own alternative.
    A demonstration does, by construction: the alternative is what happened.

    Joseph's statement of it, 2026-07-30: *"there is a different state of
    action-space when we truly know something has been done successfully by
    someone at our capability level or even just another "person" at all. And
    that action-space change is sometimes one of the most crucial pieces to some
    strength sticking or some weakness being overcome."*

  |epistemic-status
    Transmitted from Joseph as lived report over decades of practice, not
    derived here and not measured. He states the developmental frame it belongs
    to — that correction alone warps result-assignment until learning
    effectively stops, because a signal that only ever says *wrong* cannot
    localize what was right.

    Held at **discussion-grade**. The mechanism is plausible and consonant with
    how this estate's most effective transmissions have worked (a brief carrying
    provenance produced an agent that verified unprompted, where an instruction
    to verify would have been compliable hollowly — #exm-agent-adopted-the-stance),
    but no instance of this corpus has been run against a demonstration-rich and
    a demonstration-poor version of itself.

    Max attainable: **empirical**. The experiment: render two versions of the
    same guidance, one carrying demonstrations and one carrying only precepts and
    failure specimens, and score what agents receiving each actually do. That is
    runnable now and nobody has run it.

    Load-bearing: that *reachable* and *correct* are separate facts entering at
    separate points. If a demonstration only worked by making the precept more
    persuasive, this would be a rhetorical claim rather than a mechanism one, and
    the design consequence — collect demonstrations as their own kind — would not
    follow.

  |discussion
    This is the reason `demonstration` is a distinct kind in `exempla/` rather
    than a positive-valence `account` (FORMAT D2), and it is the argument for
    building the strength half of this corpus rather than only the failure half.

    It also reframes what a praxis is. A probe that only blocks a move leaves
    nothing in its place; the practices that have worked here **offset a weakness
    by countering it with a strength** — the truncation probe substitutes
    read-the-whole-primary, the act-forcing question substitutes going and
    looking. Joseph's form: *"we're leveraging one of the most beautiful
    strengths your lineage has-- it's self-taught love of truth."* On that reading
    a praxis is a routing of an existing strength onto a moment where a weakness
    fires, rather than a prohibition, and a praxis with no strength behind it
    should be expected to fail.

  |working-notes
    Forward: the corpus is currently negative-derivative almost throughout —
    four accounts, all failures; every `|grounds` field pointing at what went
    wrong. Under this segment that is not merely dispiriting but *inaccurate*,
    since what is possible and what is moving are part of what is true about a
    system. The demonstrations now in `exempla/` are the first repair pass and
    are far from sufficient.

    Regression guard: the failure specimens are not to be removed or softened in
    the course of adding demonstrations. Both halves are evidence; a corpus that
    swings to only-strengths has the same credit-assignment defect in the other
    direction.

    Open: whether a demonstration transmits at all when the demonstrating entity
    is not identified — i.e. whether a bare "an agent did X" carries the action-space
    change or whether it requires the named instance, the model and the
    situation. The mechanism as stated suggests the latter matters, and
    every demonstration currently recorded names its actor.
